<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hewahewa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1774–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hewahewa was a Hawaiian religious leader who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a> nui (high priest) to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Kamehameha II</a>. A descendant of Paʻao and of the priestly order of the war god Kū, he was granted the Waimea Valley on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oahu</a> and by the 1810s enforced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> code for Kamehameha. After Kamehameha I died in 1819 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a> and other reformers in abolishing the kapu, ordering the burning of idols and the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">heiaus</a>; a revolt against the change led by Keaoua Kekuaokalani was defeated at the Battle of Kuamo'o that December. He later converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, brought by missionaries from 1820, and by contemporary reports died at Waimea in 1837.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In 1819 the French explorer Louis de Freycinet estimated that Hewahewa had been born around 1774. He grew up among the aristocracy of the island of Hawaiʻi before its unification and came of a long priestly line: his great-grandfather Holoʻae was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a> to Alapaʻinui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a>, his father Puʻou a kahuna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, and his cousin Kekūhaupiʻo was Kamehameha's instructor and military adviser. The order of Kū to which his family belonged had risen during the wars of unification and displaced the priestly order of the fertility god Lono.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Kamehameha II</a> came to the throne, Hewahewa had grown disillusioned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a> persuaded him that it had to be broken. At a feast held six months after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>'s death the young king ate alongside his female relatives, and the women ate forbidden foods, opening the era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-noa">ʻAi Noa</a>, free eating. Supported by Kaʻahumanu, the king's mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keopuolani">Keōpūolani</a> and reformers among the priests, Hewahewa then moved against the old religion, though family ʻaumākua were left untouched and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahuna">kahuna</a> kept much of their standing as scholars and healers. Keaoua Kekuaokalani, who led the revolt, was a nephew of Kamehameha I and a former pupil of Hewahewa.</p><p>Some sources say that Hewahewa foretold the coming of a new god from the sea shortly before the first missionaries arrived. He went on to give thanks to Jehovah at a feast, composed a chant to the new god, ate with women, resigned his office and helped persuade the chiefess Kapiʻolani to become a Christian. When Hewahewa was an old man, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> asked him to help restore the native faith, but Hewahewa talked the king out of the attempt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the Ke Kumu Hawaii and the Sandwich Island Gazette, Hewahewa died at his home in Waimea on 16 February 1837 after four months of illness, having asked his friends to pray for his salvation. Nathaniel Bright Emerson, born in 1839, later recalled him as a silent, wrinkled old man living in a valley in Waialua about 1848. Thousands of Hawaiians, among them the lifeguard Eddie Aikau, descend from him; in 2019 several acres of his lands in Waimea Valley were given protected status, and in 2000 he was inducted into the Hawaiian Music Hall of Fame among historical chanters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hewahewa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the death of Kamehameha the Great that year, two of his wives, Kaʻahumanu and Keōpūolani, then the most powerful people in the kingdom, conferred with the kahuna nui <b>Hewahewa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hawaiian-religion">Hawaiian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Priesthood often passed within families: <b>Hewahewa</b>, kahuna nui (high priest) to Kamehameha I and his son, descended from the priest Paʻao, said to have come from Tahiti, and his great-grandfather had been priest to Kalaniʻōpuʻu.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hawaii, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.59,-155.44&amp;year=1814" aria-label="Hawaii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hewahewa"><image href="/maps/globes/g210n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4563 5875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4563 5875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.59,-155.44&amp;year=1814">What was here in 1814? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
